# Tessera stdlib: SignalCaster
#
# A condition variable for an exclusive access, in one U32: a sequence number that every
# wake changes. A waiter reads it while it holds the access, gives it back, and sleeps
# (wait_while: a thread sleeps, a fiber parks) while it still holds what it read, so a wake that comes between the
# give-back and the sleep has changed it and the sleep returns at once: no wake
# is lost. A wait may also return without a wake, so a waiter checks its
# condition again in a loop. The same design as Rust's futex condition
# variable, the same on every operating system. It starts with seq 0 and needs
# nothing to free.

module Hosted

### A condition variable: threads wait on it with an exclusive access held until another thread changes what they wait
### for and wakes them. A waiter holds the access, checks its condition, and calls {SignalCaster.wait} while it doesn't
### hold, again after every return: a wait may return without a wake, and another thread may have taken what it was
### woken for. A waker changes the condition with the access held, then calls {SignalCaster.cast_one} or
### {SignalCaster.cast_all}, with the access held or after giving it back. No wake is lost: a waiter that checked before the change is woken.
### One U32 that every wake changes. It starts at 0, needs nothing to free, and works the same on every operating
### system.
record SignalCaster
    seq: U32

### A condition with no waiters.
### :returns: A {SignalCaster} with seq 0.
common routine SignalCaster.construct() -> Me
    c: Me = Me(seq: 0)
    return c

### Gives `access` back, sleeps until a wake, and takes it again before returning: the caller holds it afterward as
### before. It may return without a wake, so the caller checks its condition again in a loop.
### :typeparam T: The type of the value the access covers.
### :param access: The exclusive access the caller holds, which guards the condition.
@track_caller
routine @SignalCaster.wait[T](access: Authorized[T]) -> None
needs T: typename
    seen: U32 = me.seq.peek_unbroken()
    AccessManager.give_back(at: access.getview())
    wait_while(at: me.seq, expected: seen)
    AccessManager.take(
        request: AccessRequest(at: access.getview(), size: sizeof[T](), mode: ACCESS_WRITE),
        deadline: Duration.MAX, place: caller_location())
    return

### As {SignalCaster.wait}, but gives up `timeout` after the call (it may wait a little longer, down to
### the operating system's clock unit, never forever). It takes `access` again before returning either way.
### :typeparam T: The type of the value the access covers.
### :param access: The exclusive access the caller holds, which guards the condition.
### :param timeout: The longest wait.
### :returns: Whether it returned before the timeout passed: false means the time is up, true means woken (or
### returned without a wake) in time, and the caller checks its condition again.
@track_caller
routine @SignalCaster.wait_for[T](access: Authorized[T], timeout: Duration) -> Bool
needs T: typename
    deadline: Duration = Clock.monotonic() +^ timeout
    return me.wait_until[T](access, deadline)

### As {SignalCaster.wait}, but gives up at `deadline` on the {Clock.monotonic} clock. A loop that waits for a condition
### keeps one deadline across its waits, so the wakes that don't satisfy it don't extend the wait. It takes `access`
### again before returning either way, and gives it back even when the deadline has already passed.
### :typeparam T: The type of the value the access covers.
### :param access: The exclusive access the caller holds, which guards the condition.
### :param deadline: When to give up, in {Clock.monotonic} time.
### :returns: Whether it returned before the deadline: false means the time is up, true means woken (or returned
### without a wake) in time, and the caller checks its condition again.
@track_caller
routine @SignalCaster.wait_until[T](access: Authorized[T], deadline: Duration) -> Bool
needs T: typename
    seen: U32 = me.seq.peek_unbroken()
    AccessManager.give_back(at: access.getview())
    now: Duration = Clock.monotonic()
    left: Duration = deadline -^ now
    wait_while_for(at: me.seq, expected: seen, timeout: left)
    AccessManager.take(
        request: AccessRequest(at: access.getview(), size: sizeof[T](), mode: ACCESS_WRITE),
        deadline: Duration.MAX, place: caller_location())
    return Clock.monotonic() < deadline

### Wakes one thread waiting on the condition, if any. Call it after changing the condition with the access held.
routine @SignalCaster.cast_one() -> None
    me.seq.fetch_add_unbroken(1)
    wake_one(me.seq)
    return

### Wakes every thread waiting on the condition. Call it after changing the condition with the access held.
routine @SignalCaster.cast_all() -> None
    me.seq.fetch_add_unbroken(1)
    wake_all(me.seq)
    return
